When a pair of dice is thrown, the total number of outcomes is \(6 \times 6 = 36\).
A 'success' is defined as getting a total of 5. The favorable outcomes for a success are:
\{(1, 4), (2, 3), (3, 2), (4, 1)\}
The number of favorable outcomes is 4.
The probability of success (p) in a single throw is:
p = P(\text{success}) = \frac{\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \frac{4}{36} = \frac{1}{9}
The probability of failure (q) is:
q = 1 - p = 1 - \frac{1}{9} = \frac{8}{9}
The experiment is repeated 5 times, so this is a binomial distribution with \(n=5\) and \(p = \frac{1}{9}\).
The probability of at least 4 successes is \(P(X \ge 4) = P(X=4) + P(X=5)\).
P(X \ge 4) = {}^{5}C_{4} p^{4} q^{1} + {}^{5}C_{5} p^{5} q^{0}
= 5 \left(\frac{1}{9}\right)^{4} \left(\frac{8}{9}\right) + 1 \left(\frac{1}{9}\right)^{5}
= \frac{5 \times 8}{9^{5}} + \frac{1}{9^{5}} = \frac{40+1}{9^{5}} = \frac{41}{9^{5}}
We are given that this probability is equal to \(\frac{k}{3^{11}}\).
\frac{41}{9^{5}} = \frac{k}{3^{11}}
Since \(9 = 3^2\), we can write \(9^5 = (3^2)^5 = 3^{10}\).
\frac{41}{3^{10}} = \frac{k}{3^{11}}
Solving for k:
k = 41 \times \frac{3^{11}}{3^{10}} = 41 \times 3^{11-10} = 41 \times 3 = 123

